public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    private static final String HOT_RATING = 'Hot';
    private static final String HIGH_PRIORITY = 'High';

    public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<Case> newCases) {
        // 1. Collect the account Ids referenced by the new cases.
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. One query for all accounts, outside the loop (bulk-safe).
        Map<Id, Account> hotAccounts = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds AND Rating = :HOT_RATING]
        );

        if (hotAccounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 3. Set priority on cases whose account is Hot.
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null && hotAccounts.containsKey(c.AccountId)) {
                c.Priority = HIGH_PRIORITY;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

One note: `with sharing` means the account lookup respects the running user's record access. If Email-to-Case or integration users cannot see some accounts, those cases would not be bumped to High. If the rule should apply regardless of visibility, change it to `without sharing`.

Calling the ERP directly inside the trigger isn't possible, and strict one-request-per-account runs into a platform limit. Both are handled below.

**Deviations**

1. **The callout is not made inline in the trigger.** Apex doesn't allow a synchronous callout from trigger context, because the DML transaction is still open. The trigger enqueues a Queueable (`Database.AllowsCallouts`), which makes the callouts right after the commit. It still happens as soon as the account is created, just asynchronously.
2. **Each Queueable job sends at most 50 accounts, and the rest are chained to a follow-up job.** A transaction may make at most 100 callouts, so 200 accounts at one request each can't fit in a single transaction. The ERP also accepts an array, but I kept one request per account (a one-element array) so the ERP team can trace every account on its own, as you asked. A batch of 200 therefore runs as 4 chained jobs, and a single user-created account runs as 1 job with 1 callout. I chose 50 rather than 100 to stay well inside the 120 s cumulative callout time limit.
3. **The trigger passes a snapshot of Id, Name and BillingCountry to the job instead of re-querying.** This avoids sharing or FLS surprises and an extra query.
4. **Error handling and retries are out of scope.** I only catch `CalloutException` per account, so one failed call doesn't stop the remaining accounts in the job. Failures are written to the debug log only.
5. **Tests are not included.** Chained Queueables can't be enqueued from a Queueable in an Apex test, so a test with more than 50 accounts needs the chain-depth limit accounted for.

