public with sharing class RecordAccessService {

    // SOQL on UserRecordAccess allows at most 200 record IDs per query.
    @TestVisible
    private static final Integer CHUNK_SIZE = 200;

    public static Set<Id> editableIds(Set<Id> recordIds) {
        Set<Id> result = new Set<Id>();
        if (recordIds == null || recordIds.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        Id userId = UserInfo.getUserId();
        List<Id> chunk = new List<Id>();

        for (Id recordId : recordIds) {
            if (recordId == null) {
                continue;
            }
            chunk.add(recordId);
            if (chunk.size() == CHUNK_SIZE) {
                collectEditable(userId, chunk, result);
                chunk = new List<Id>();
            }
        }
        if (!chunk.isEmpty()) {
            collectEditable(userId, chunk, result);
        }
        return result;
    }

    private static void collectEditable(Id userId, List<Id> chunk, Set<Id> result) {
        for (UserRecordAccess ura : [
            SELECT RecordId, HasEditAccess
            FROM UserRecordAccess
            WHERE UserId = :userId
            AND RecordId IN :chunk
        ]) {
            if (ura.HasEditAccess) {
                result.add(ura.RecordId);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The class sends the IDs to `UserRecordAccess` in chunks of 200, because that object accepts at most 200 record IDs per query. For 2,000 IDs that is 10 SOQL queries, within the limit of 20. I did not group IDs by object type, since that could push the query count over 20 when many object types are mixed. I'm relying on `UserRecordAccess` accepting IDs from different objects in one query, which I believe it does but have not run against an org.

